{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785331729,"nonce":"SEZJooTpRZsENYdazRuxUaaJhoI=","message":"Re-verification on 2026-07-29 shows the mechanism in the earlier RCA is\nwrong. cgit does NOT return 200 for missing commits — a truly absent\nobject gets a proper error status from git.kernel.org (400 for an\nunresolvable short id, 404 for a full-length nonexistent id), and the\nexisting `status_code \u003e= 300` gate catches both. This is why the\nreporter's second observation (\"DTRT if the commit hasn't been pushed at\nall\") holds: not-pushed-anywhere commits stay queued correctly.\n\n\ngit.kernel.org repositories are grokmirror-managed and same-family repos\nshare an object store via alternates. cgit resolves `/commit/?id=`\ndirectly from the odb — alternates included — with **no reachability\ncheck**. Once a commit has been pushed to any sibling repo sharing the\nobjstore, the cgit commit URL for a *different* repo in that family\nreturns **HTTP 200 with a fully rendered commit page**, even though no\nref in that repo can reach the commit.\n\nVerified with a live test case (2026-07-29): the commit\n7f102800164990609e2b272396d45b197a55bd04 exists only in a fork sharing\nobjects with torvalds/linux.git, not in torvalds/linux.git itself, yet\n\n    https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/commit/?id=7f102800164990609e2b272396d45b197a55bd04\n\nreturns 200 for HEAD and GET, with both browser-like and `b4/x.y`\nUser-Agent strings, and renders the full commit page.\n\nThis matches the reporter's workflow exactly: the series was pushed to\nci.git (unpublished sibling) first, its objects reached the shared\nobjstore, and the misc.git checkurl answered 200, so `process_queue()`\nfell through and sent the thanks.\n\n\n`process_queue()` in `src/b4/ty.py:1080-1093`:\n\n    resp = session.head(checkurl, timeout=15, allow_redirects=True)\n    if resp.status_code \u003e= 300:\n        still_pending += 1\n        ...\n        continue\n    # falls through to send\n\nTwo observations about why no variant of this HTTP check can express\n\"published\":\n\n1. The failing response is a **legitimate 200 commit page** — there is no\n   error status to key on.\n2. cgit does emit a body marker for this state (\"Notice: this object is\n   not reachable from any branch.\") but the code issues a HEAD request\n   and never sees a body; and the marker is cgit-specific presentation\n   text in any case.\n\nThe rest of the earlier RCA stands: the checkurl is built correctly from\n`thanks-commit-url-mask` against the last taken commit\n(`_tracking_app.py:4879-4889`), stored as the `X-Check-URL` header\n(`ty.py:964-966`), and the non-queued path (`_send_thank_message`,\n`_tracking_app.py:4944`) performs no publish check at all.\n\n\n- Proven: the shared-objstore 200 mechanism (live test case above);\n  400/404 for truly missing objects; the code path.\n- Inferred only: in the original March report, the reporter's browser\n  showed \"Bad object\" for the same URL around the time b4 must have\n  received a 200. Reconciling that requires objstore-migration timing or\n  multi-node replication skew between git.kernel.org frontends. The core\n  mechanism does not depend on this detail.\n\n\n77d45c3 (scriptable queue processing outside the TUI) is coupled in\npractice: automating the queue kick raises the stakes on this gate being\ntrustworthy.","files":null}]}